review / review	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-08-15T04:56:08.6254955Z - **task_toml_schema:** `[task].description` (line 6) is not among the fields the rubric enumerates as recognized; it is benign and commonly present, so I graded PASS. If the reviewing team treats any non-enumerated field as extraneous, this would flip to FAIL — confirming Harbor's accepted `[task]` sub-fields would settle it.
review / review	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-08-15T04:56:08.6256644Z - **solvable / test values:** Because the `verification_explanation` was clearly not updated when orders O00_F–O00_M were added, there is residual doubt that every one of the 19 hardcoded test values was re-validated against `solve.py`. This is read-only, so I could not execute to confirm; the solution is a genuine generic simulator, so I graded solvable PASS. Running `solve.py` against the generated DB and diffing its `report.json` against the 19 hardcoded assertions would confirm or refute alignment and would also settle any borderline concern on `test_instruction_alignment` and `reviewable`.
review / review	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-08-15T04:56:08.6258115Z - **outcome_verified** is borderline-detailed (the instruction specifies steps 1–6 of the algorithm), but these define the problem's non-standard business semantics needed for deterministic verification rather than mandating tools or a command procedure, so it passes.
